#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07e8cbe2-1fe8-5038-9b0a-061070ffe1fb" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions With Equal Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "ae470aa4-327d-5b21-9e11-217710f1ef94" :question/math-academy-id "e-13787" :question/problem "![](images/91/916d8a6c0c4ba6c249191c0ee0d85d20f3e14ebfbabbdb0a6a5be583e39be139.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea208841c3276de2e8736add1ad5c6f4c343a9ec8d137f74f10e47b9243e87eb.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ fits into $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{8}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times ${\\color{blue}3}$ fits into ${\\color{red}5}.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$, we can simply compute ${\\color{red}5}\\div {\\color{blue}3}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fae4d19-7336-5557-8df9-bc8e0b6703fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Missing Number"}] :question/id #uuid "0b51ee70-1d44-5427-8b9f-3930dc3a1962" :question/math-academy-id "e-13790" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e7367d6b3a53b513c2921d91579b1bd6588b850622ecf1c7b370695227a64d.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n![](images/e8/e8b1d1d44a8b8b08fa31061f8a87e632a8422f3494d77d9b6d8ba02a446c3e65.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $5$ and $10.$ We can make a common denominator of $10$ by splitting the shape on the left into $2$ parts vertically:  \n\n![](images/3d/3d4839662bcac31bc197436b533da61e07ecf1ca301b3fa7613d433833e14028.png)\n\n \n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{10} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} \\div {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](images/58/583d7875ca78bb24742c402e222b4ba221388163d17f2056372e204d27373043.svg)\n\nHence, the missing number is $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab62b651-0d87-5ed3-b749-11d1e25be6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions Using Models"}] :question/id #uuid "daa61f81-4882-5af9-b86a-8b03a586e93b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13792" :question/problem "![](images/87/87ce51fed33e907d6acb7e213bec596ea97b16e739bb96b5446975dc6a593011.png)\n\nCalculate $\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}$ using the model above." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $4$ and $3.$ We can make a common denominator of $4\\times 3 = 12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/93/931f2220f4f5b4e8d3760bcf283d19ef565d2bd8910db6d95cd44e604782a0f0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{12} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{9} \\, .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50b35123-63ff-559f-b9b6-61ea6915ce0a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "066a9329-d63e-55d4-bc6b-bcdb5dd96cc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13793" :question/problem "![](images/93/93a73e4b7458251fdb96891e2b00af4fd5ecf6a442aed9fb7c289ca25ae0e389.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $7$ and $14.$ We can make a common denominator of $14.$\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/a9/a9867f3e62605ccbaad6fdc6a1925173a460f287e36b8cc563ab62bb3074a13c.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{14}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}11} \\div {\\color{blue}6} = 1\\,\\dfrac56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = 1\\,\\dfrac56 \\, .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab62b651-0d87-5ed3-b749-11d1e25be6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions Using Models"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198d3c0a-e01d-5316-a03b-b1cb56b75c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477779} {:answer/id #uuid "30df9e05-1c6a-515e-be1d-f43f91d53bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477825} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6b0389-71ee-5111-a995-0ba6dae8a5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477991} {:answer/id #uuid "7e516a5c-7ce2-5aae-bfcf-65bfe9d7a65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477996} {:answer/id #uuid "c357f95d-98d4-50b2-8545-1da210f9d6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30df9e05-1c6a-515e-be1d-f43f91d53bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477825} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e27c43b-c731-5047-8f0b-ee46131f38a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "615e7b46-802a-503e-b549-f33b6cd0168c" :question/math-academy-id "q-100720" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6cdbde09d3f5f1965b6de9798116331513bc3a7ee00a5784a5934eefbec0ed42.png)\n\nFind the value of $\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{3}{4}$ given the model above." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $5$ and $4.$ We can make a common denominator of $5\\times 4=20.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $5$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/d4/d456f6fe7acc94b5a9e8e366fb2faea3ec2683e731a5e4cdafcdcbf6ce685bd2.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{20}÷\\frac{15}{20}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n12÷15=\\frac{12}{15}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{12}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07e8cbe2-1fe8-5038-9b0a-061070ffe1fb" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions With Equal Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e17e6081-cea8-594f-96bf-e0f4e171ca50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e17e6081-cea8-594f-96bf-e0f4e171ca50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478179} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf420fd-ea28-5c7e-8346-2d9198108ae1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9451a4b-084f-5269-a43e-e06464c25eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-222766" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9884bf61d9b904b3e6846e6a1d307e34e1b84023e8e5a321c09be6bfd44069c.png)\n\nUse the model above to compute the following result. Express your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.\n\n$\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/32/327c59257b6ea1d0b18f73bcff065a8ddade49db9ce6b84ba44c824d1c041901.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\frac{3}{5}$ fits into $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times $3$ fits into $1.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}$, we can simply compute $1÷3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n1÷3=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}÷\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fae4d19-7336-5557-8df9-bc8e0b6703fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Missing Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496d3325-647b-5cfd-9427-60a7a7f19d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496d3325-647b-5cfd-9427-60a7a7f19d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477879} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e866461-304a-5092-9672-7c84dc247b77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05c4dc30-e572-5db4-9e5c-4a5cd61df427" :question/math-academy-id "q-222925" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a583013eef64a37770bae73f1b17d0df310e1e290e44f1f8a500ccd543346d51.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"1\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\">$÷$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"25\">$=$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"25\" width=\"25\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $4$ and $2.$ We can make a common denominator of $4$ by splitting the shape on the right into $2$ parts vertically:\n\n![](images/c4/c401472d271b22b1461c6f02888196a29b6b5b1d069d52638c325e807caab8c0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{4}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n3÷2=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab62b651-0d87-5ed3-b749-11d1e25be6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions Using Models"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "606fc75a-b4a5-5666-a99a-e0154c3871e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "606fc75a-b4a5-5666-a99a-e0154c3871e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477932} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d8bafc-6060-5c9b-bab8-328b2d8dae54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e84b31c0-fab3-5cf9-b831-412b917ad955" :question/math-academy-id "q-222987" :question/problem "![](images/21/21d3c3c28f9ef10e5ac2623724ead9593ba24dbb1f94b81ce5313f94ee7ca511.png)\n\nUse the model above to compute the following result. Express your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.\n\n$\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{6}{7}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $4$ and $7.$ We can make a common denominator of $4\\times 7=28.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $7$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $4$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/91/919b2e3f87aabf375cb968e876ee19b0ce9cd6a70c41088b062459a1f4f1f133.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{28}÷\\frac{24}{28}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n21÷24=\\frac{21}{24}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{6}{7}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50b35123-63ff-559f-b9b6-61ea6915ce0a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ff89363-959b-5357-8004-bd6070af1837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477795} {:answer/id #uuid "3d42f1d4-3d13-59e6-9488-44375b5b12e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477849} {:answer/id #uuid "b8033fe8-08b2-5de5-a1c0-e1f2ebb8542a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478096} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd735d2-5a0f-59d8-83b2-af390b19e5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478175} {:answer/id #uuid "f774af8c-e084-5779-ac30-9abe76a7348b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d42f1d4-3d13-59e6-9488-44375b5b12e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477849} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63e0a96-aa3c-51a2-85f6-28ecd55a49ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53eb3b3f-e5cc-5f81-bd02-ccecc2d8b701" :question/math-academy-id "q-61395" :question/problem "![](images/10/108b7e775bdbd2422c87691b3ea68a82aa68ee925d5dc01517ac7327bba5959f.png)\n\nCalculate $\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{3}$ using the model above." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $3$ and $4.$ We can make a common denominator of $12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $3$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $4$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/1e/1e7a163bb4ee9efb6574e55a37ddeeac3503f2b2b873ba7ba253a969c92d3bb7.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{12}÷\\frac{8}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n9÷8=1\\,\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{3}=1\\,\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07e8cbe2-1fe8-5038-9b0a-061070ffe1fb" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions With Equal Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a4eaa37-377a-5929-824f-d428d1a42dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142258} {:answer/id #uuid "a558181a-3e97-5894-99e2-c235d37c28a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142259} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccfae68-63c2-55d1-91fa-6fe2f48b8975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142260} {:answer/id #uuid "4db0e950-b0cc-5ae9-88d6-8c086cf234e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142261} {:answer/id #uuid "57463895-e1e1-5e01-a309-3e43dcaf447e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a4eaa37-377a-5929-824f-d428d1a42dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142258} :answer-field/id #uuid "e99e3d66-90e7-51d9-bb59-35aae7b7a7b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186142257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "111d2c11-f19a-5d34-ba2c-4cfd8712b8f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-61417" :question/problem "![](images/57/57382cd8235f5b141e5414492d3375178da41136f547620b1dd7acfc664b158b.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/24/24e777c7b9029007debe066de66ea7041b96d02702788bfea4d2da4914a47da6.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\frac{3}{4}$ fits into $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times $3$ fits into $1.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}$, we can simply compute $1÷3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n1÷3=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50b35123-63ff-559f-b9b6-61ea6915ce0a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028d092b-7c92-56a1-9945-c1a3dc7075f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477739} {:answer/id #uuid "18762c31-a5ef-5bf4-b3d8-58904d9d0729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477778} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba4f310-379a-5b47-8b00-14d9b667593a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477887} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8690f8-aa06-57af-8222-9bcf50a61b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477896} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d3cf68-5f43-57f4-8a6d-def212a7a6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2d3cf68-5f43-57f4-8a6d-def212a7a6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478118} :answer-field/id #uuid "479cd153-296c-56e1-acf7-5d309027bd92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92d12b02-6a27-52b9-bae0-b7994bcddd77" :question/math-academy-id "q-61482" :question/problem "![](images/82/82ee6742675576eb252a624a1089b3f77f3bf9a4db3be00798799f5380150173.png)\n\nCalculate $\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{3}{6}$ using the model above." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $4$ and $6.$ We can make a common denominator of $12.$ \n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $3$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $4$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/2b/2b342d9977254cd8af8fb93fe632f4c154b6f797e3fcb89bdfc91945f7736dd1.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{12}÷\\frac{6}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n9÷6=1\\,\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{3}{6}=1\\,\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50b35123-63ff-559f-b9b6-61ea6915ce0a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a437082-afcb-5fd5-812a-2e65149204ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477755} {:answer/id #uuid "15c3bbe4-ef24-51e3-b4ea-c5ba2e0c63b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477774} {:answer/id #uuid "5376b9cf-b7bd-58a4-b976-c4dde31da83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477907} {:answer/id #uuid "69b09f16-eaff-5da6-83f9-5c5da178c727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477949} {:answer/id #uuid "df2e5845-8d5c-509f-8cd0-54a5804b8464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2e5845-8d5c-509f-8cd0-54a5804b8464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478174} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5bc40c-1ad1-5d4f-bb62-661567f48445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cab23e35-9f7a-52cc-9e73-2a48af990f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-61604" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b5c4781d474dffc32a48a6d9b814e8181dd73b596ba05828d918fa42a3916ab.png)\n\nCalculate $\\frac{7}{10}÷\\frac{3}{5}$ using the model above." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $5$ and $10.$ We can make a common denominator of $10.$\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts vertically:\n\n![](images/43/43424ef31fc44fa8485ee7a7b5a0ce6855858ad477d8365ac2ad898d25353f29.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{10}÷\\frac{6}{10}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n7÷6=1\\,\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{10}÷\\frac{3}{5}=1\\,\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fae4d19-7336-5557-8df9-bc8e0b6703fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Missing Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9e3253-4491-595f-afa5-cc74d18543f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477814} {:answer/id #uuid "4723aec4-8a34-5dbe-87ad-beb90931b3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477874} {:answer/id #uuid "594cd235-409f-5c95-95c6-6f8252118105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477918} {:answer/id #uuid "5959f05f-41b6-5388-949b-96464cbe98ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477919} {:answer/id #uuid "d3864e81-f801-52be-81d3-8fdab371bd58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3864e81-f801-52be-81d3-8fdab371bd58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478154} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dd2005d-f78c-5253-b095-60c2aa5727e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c24b98e-30b0-587e-a562-0c053d2d0ee7" :question/math-academy-id "q-92662" :question/problem "![](images/14/14f6306057655315d9de48645c14c25c839f322500dcf599911c4b8d9deb637b.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{12}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{9}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $12$ and $4.$ We can make a common denominator of $12$ by splitting the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/1d/1d05074d23c48a2ff9604d5a1aec5521c0fb043ebeed9424f7182e480404771d.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{12}÷\\frac{9}{12}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n7÷9=\\frac{7}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{12}÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{\\boxed{\\,7\\,}}{9}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $7.$"}]}
